is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Routing control plane (RIP) on router4. The LAN at Location C (192.168.200.0/24) is not being advertised into the inter-site routing domain.

Mechanism: Routers across the WAN exchange reachability via RIP. Routers 0–3 and 5 have active RIP and know many remote prefixes. However, router4’s RIP process is configured with no “network” statements, so it does not advertise its directly connected 192.168.200.0/24 (Location C LAN). As a result, from Location A (172.16.10.0/24 behind router0), there is no route to 192.168.200.0/24. This manifests as intermittent-looking reachability because adjacent infrastructure at C (e.g., router4’s 192.168.15.2) is reachable and traceroutes may progress partway, but host IPs in 192.168.200.0/24 are consistently unknown to the rest of the network.

Localization evidence:
- router4: FRR running-config shows “router rip” with no networks configured.
- router0/1/2/3/5: “show ip route 192.168.200.0/24” returns “Network not in table.”
- From pc4 (172.16.10.4), ping to 192.168.200.11 gets “Destination Net Unreachable” from 172.16.10.1 (router0), confirming the missing route. pc4 can reach 192.168.15.2 (router4 eth4), proving the WAN path toward router4 exists.

Root cause: RIP is not enabled on router4’s interfaces, so 192.168.200.0/24 is never advertised to other sites.

Corrective fix: On router4, enable RIP v2 on the relevant networks or redistribute connected, e.g.:
- router rip
- version 2
- network 192.168.200.0/24
- network 192.168.15.0/24
- network 172.25.0.0/16
Then “write memory.” This will install 192.168.200.0/24 across the WAN, making Location C consistently reachable from Location A. === END ===